2. Make controller customization genuinely complete

The current controller system already supports per-fighter customization, normalized PlayStation/Xbox buttons, single-button inputs, modifiers, directional conditions, and different activation types.

Preserve these capabilities while removing unnecessary restrictions and extending the system.

The objective is to allow players to build highly personalized controller layouts without being restricted to the game’s recommended control philosophy.

2.1 Full right-joystick customization

The right joystick must become a fully configurable input source, with capabilities comparable to the left joystick.

It must not be limited to functioning as an alternative movement source.

Players should be able to configure how the right joystick participates in gameplay, including independent directional inputs and combinations with other controls where appropriate.

Investigate and implement support for:

* Configuring the left and right joysticks independently.
* Recognizing directional movement from either joystick.
* Binding supported gameplay actions to individual joystick directions.
* Using either joystick’s directional state as part of a combined input.
* Supporting appropriate press, hold, and continuous behaviors for joystick-driven actions.
* Allowing one joystick to handle movement while the other performs separately configured actions.
* Correctly handling simultaneous input from both joysticks.
* Supporting joystick-based actions independently of the currently selected movement source.
* Applying appropriate deadzones, directional thresholds, edge detection, and neutral-position resetting.
* Preventing unintended actions from stick drift, slight analog movements, or repeated activation while a stick is held.
* Giving users clear visual feedback showing exactly which joystick and direction are assigned to each action.

Where directional stick movement triggers an action, ensure that the resulting behavior is intentional, predictable, and context-aware.

A directional action should not repeatedly activate simply because the player continues holding the stick unless continuous activation is specifically appropriate.

Investigate the existing movementVector, stickDirection, directionMet, directionHeld, compileProfile, and ControllerResolver implementations.

The current implementation derives directional conditions from the selected movement source. Remove the architectural coupling where it prevents independent joystick configuration.

Design an extensible input representation that explicitly distinguishes the relevant joystick, direction, activation, and any modifiers.

Both joysticks must remain fully usable together without interfering with each other.

2.2 Allow customization of every controller button

Every physical controller button exposed by the supported Gamepad API mapping should be eligible for customization.

This includes all supported categories of inputs:

* Face buttons.
* Shoulder buttons.
* Analog triggers.
* Directional pad inputs.
* Joystick-click buttons.
* Menu and secondary buttons exposed by the browser.
* Both joysticks’ supported directional inputs.

Do not artificially exclude an input simply because the current recommended layout or existing input system uses it for a particular function.

Allow players to reassign gameplay actions using the complete available input vocabulary.

Preserve compatibility with ALVA’s supported PlayStation and Xbox controller families and their normalized button representation.

Respect the actual capabilities and limitations of the browser’s Gamepad API. Do not claim that inaccessible hardware, operating-system-reserved inputs, or controller-specific extra buttons are remappable when they cannot be reliably detected.

2.3 Customization must include universal actions

Every gameplay action available through the controller should be customizable.

This includes movement, jumping, Dash/air dash, fast fall, defensive actions, the fighter’s actual attacks, Special abilities, and relevant system actions.

Users must not be forced to retain ALVA’s recommended universal bindings.

Distinguish between two concepts:

* Recommended layouts: Universal actions must use consistent recommended controls across every fighter.
* Custom layouts: Players must remain free to override those recommended inputs individually.

A player customizing one fighter must not unintentionally alter another fighter’s saved profile.

2.4 Preserve and improve the binding editor

Expand the existing Controller controls editor rather than introducing a disconnected customization system.

The editor should make advanced customization understandable even for players who are unfamiliar with controller remapping.

Ensure the interface clearly communicates:

* Which action is being edited.
* Which physical input triggers it.
* Whether the input comes from the left or right joystick.
* Any required joystick direction.
* Any button modifiers.
* The action’s activation behavior.
* Whether the binding applies on the ground, in the air, or both.
* Any conflicts with other bindings.

Maintain both methods of customization: directly detecting controller input and manually selecting an input through the interface.

Players without a connected controller should still be able to configure their layouts.

Update the controller diagram so either joystick can be highlighted independently, including directional indications.

Keep input labels correct for the currently selected controller family.

Make the editing experience straightforward. The additional flexibility must not make basic customization confusing or cumbersome.

2.5 Conflicts and input resolution

Reevaluate ALVA’s binding conflict detection.

It must correctly understand independent joystick sources, physical buttons, modifiers, activation modes, and gameplay context.

Avoid false conflicts where two bindings can safely coexist.

Also prevent genuinely ambiguous configurations from silently activating the wrong action.

Preserve and improve contextual input reuse, including the distinction between ground and aerial actions.

A binding conflict is not determined solely by whether two bindings mention the same button. It depends on whether their complete activation conditions can overlap in actual gameplay.

Ensure simultaneous controller inputs are handled deterministically.

Preserve ALVA’s existing safety behavior concerning stale inputs, paused gameplay, disconnected controllers, fighter switching, and buffered actions.

Do not allow controller customization to create stuck movement, permanently active defenses, repeated unintended attacks, or gameplay inputs leaking out of menus.

Keep menu navigation reliable regardless of customized gameplay bindings. If menu controls themselves are made customizable, provide a dependable recovery mechanism that prevents users from locking themselves out of the interface.

