3. Redesign the recommended layouts around beginners

This is the most important game-design component of the task.

Reevaluate the recommended controller layout for every currently playable fighter.

The objective is not to create traditional fighting-game controls, preserve familiar conventions at any cost, or organize inputs according to internal attack numbers.

The objective is to create controls that make sense naturally to someone who has never played ALVA.

The player’s intuition, physical comfort, and ability to understand the move should determine the recommended input.

Every fighter must receive a carefully designed layout based on its actual moves.

3.1 Do not use arbitrary or mechanical assignment rules

Do not blindly assign moves based on:

* Their internal attack number.
* Their order in a fighter definition.
* Their damage or power alone.
* Their cooldown alone.
* Their category alone.
* Their internal animation identifier.
* The current recommended layout.
* Whether another fighter happens to have a superficially similar move.

These characteristics may inform a decision where genuinely relevant, but they must not determine the layout automatically.

Instead, investigate each move individually and choose its recommended input based on its specific mechanics, observed appearance, and practical controller ergonomics.

3.2 Prioritize the visible direction of the move

Directional intuition should be central to the recommended layout design.

However, a critical distinction must be maintained:

The visible direction of a move is not the same thing as its Directional Launch.

Directional Launch describes where the opponent is sent after being hit. It must not be mistaken for the visual trajectory, physical motion, or directional meaning of the move itself.

Analyze what the player actually sees.

Consider:

* The direction in which the fighter physically moves while executing the attack.
* The direction in which a limb, weapon, or other attacking object travels.
* Whether the fighter rises, descends, lunges, spins, dives, charges, or remains stationary.
* Whether the attack is aimed toward an opponent, an area, or a fixed direction.
* Whether the move creates a projectile, persistent object, environmental effect, or summon.
* Whether the move’s visible motion clearly communicates a directional intention.
* Whether the move’s animation changes depending on facing or other contextual information.
* Whether any visual direction is incidental rather than meaningful to the attack’s actual execution.

A move that sends its opponent upward is not necessarily an upward-moving attack.

A downward-launching attack is not necessarily a downward-moving attack.

A projectile’s travel direction, a fighter’s movement direction, and the resulting knockback direction may all be different.

Do not confuse them.

The input should reflect the most intuitive interpretation of the actual visible action and mechanic, not merely its hit result.

Also recognize when a move has no meaningful directional association. Do not force a directional input onto an otherwise straightforward action merely to make the layout appear systematic.

3.3 Evaluate the complete mechanics of every move

A move’s mechanics can dramatically change how comfortable or intuitive its input feels.

For every move, investigate:

Execution and motion: Whether it is an immediate strike, multihit sequence, moving attack, projectile, trap, summon, technique, homing maneuver, rising attack, plunge, roll, hover, healing action, or another mechanic.

Commitment: Whether the fighter can move freely during the move, whether it changes momentum, requires a charge or cast, locks the fighter in place, or introduces an extended recovery period.

Timing: Whether it requires immediate reactions, precise timing, frequent repetition, sustained holding, or deliberate activation.

Movement interaction: Whether players are likely to perform it while running, jumping, falling, turning, Dashing, or recovering near the edge of a stage.

Targeting: Whether it follows an opponent, travels according to facing, affects a surrounding area, creates an effect at a target location, or interacts with nearby objects.

Frequency and purpose: Whether it is a commonly used neutral action, a combo tool, a movement/recovery option, a defensive response, an occasional utility action, or a powerful Special ability.

Risk: Whether unintended activation could be especially disruptive, such as consuming a critical resource, interrupting movement, causing an unwanted plunge, or beginning a lengthy cast.

Visual presentation: Whether its animation offers an intuitive connection to a particular kind of input or control gesture.

Make the final recommendation through a holistic evaluation of these factors.

Do not allow one consideration to override the actual play experience without justification.

3.4 Favor physical comfort and minimal effort

Recommended controls must be physically comfortable on supported PlayStation and Xbox controllers.

Consider the player’s hands and fingers, not just the logical arrangement of inputs.

Evaluate:

* Whether the player can move and attack comfortably at the same time.
* Whether a frequent action requires repositioning a thumb unnecessarily.
* Whether a directional input conflicts physically with movement.
* Whether a combination is awkward to execute.
* Whether the player can respond quickly while already holding a movement input.
* Whether a commonly used action requires too much coordination.
* Whether the recommended inputs are comfortable during repeated use.
* Whether the controls feel natural during fast-paced combat.
* Whether players can chain movement, defense, jumps, and attacks without unnecessary hand repositioning.
* Whether adjacent actions produce an understandable physical control pattern.

Avoid unnecessary simultaneous inputs, awkward chords, and gestures that demand more coordination than the move deserves.

Basic and frequently used actions should be exceptionally accessible.

More deliberate inputs may be appropriate when the move’s actual mechanics justify them, but do not make powerful or unusual moves difficult merely because they are powerful or unusual.

Ease of execution matters more than creating artificially complicated inputs.

3.5 Create an intuitive control language

Although each fighter should receive individually designed attack bindings, the overall recommended system should feel coherent.

Look for meaningful relationships between input gestures and move behavior.

When two moves have similar visual or mechanical purposes, consider whether their inputs should share an intuitive relationship.

When their behavior differs substantially, do not force them into the same control pattern solely to preserve consistency.

Ground and aerial moves require special consideration.

The same physical input can perform different actions depending on whether the fighter is grounded or airborne, but that relationship must be understandable.

Contextual reuse can make a layout much simpler if it feels logical.

It can also confuse players if the two actions have no intuitive relationship.

Make these decisions deliberately.

Do not sacrifice a fighter’s natural control feel merely to impose identical attack arrangements across the roster.

The recommended layouts should feel related through a consistent design philosophy, not through an arbitrary universal attack template.

