7. Implement the redesigned recommendations correctly

After completing the analysis, update the actual recommended controller profiles.

The recommendation system must continue to work independently for each fighter.

Maintain ALVA’s existing character-driven architecture:

* Fighter definitions own their individual move layouts.
* Shared input systems must remain fighter-agnostic.
* Universal recommended actions must come from shared configuration rather than being copied independently into fighter-specific code.
* A fighter’s recommended layout must cover every actual bindable action it possesses.
* Unsupported actions must not appear.
* Every recommended profile must be validated.
* New fighters must remain supported by an appropriate fallback mechanism.

Update the profile validation rules if necessary to support the expanded input model.

Make sure recommended profiles cannot introduce ambiguous or unusable bindings.

If the redesigned controller architecture changes the shape of a binding, update all readers and validators consistently.

Do not introduce fighter-ID-specific exceptions into the shared input resolver.

Ensure the final recommendation is actually used by the game, not merely displayed in the editor.

