14. Definition of success

The redesign succeeds when:

A new player can pick up a controller, select any ALVA fighter, and quickly begin moving, attacking, jumping, Dashing, and defending without feeling that the controls are complicated or arbitrary.

The player should be able to recognize a move’s visual or mechanical character and find its input natural to execute.

Switching fighters should introduce new abilities, not force the player to relearn basic movement and defense.

An experienced player should then be able to customize the controller deeply—including both joysticks and every available physical button—without arbitrary limitations.

The controller configuration interface should make this freedom easy to understand rather than intimidating.

There should be no unnecessary friction between what a player intends to do and what their fingers must do.

Final guiding principle:

ALVA should not require the player to adapt to an awkward control scheme. Its recommended controls should adapt intelligently to the game’s actual moves, the physical controller, and the natural expectations of a new player.

Make the recommended layouts feel exceptionally intuitive, physically comfortable, responsive, and effortless.

Think independently, evaluate every fighter and every action, choose all bindings yourself, implement the improvements comprehensively, and verify the finished experience.

The priority is not to preserve the existing layout. The priority is to deliver the best possible controller experience for ALVA’s players.
